---
title: "WebAssembly SDK"
description: "Parse, analyze, compile, and optionally execute QQL in browsers and JavaScript runtimes."
sidebar:
  order: 5
---

{% terminal title="Install" %}
npm install qql-wasm
{% /terminal %}

{% qqlExample title="Input used below" %}
QUERY TEXT 'browser retrieval' FROM docs
USING dense
LIMIT 5;
{% /qqlExample %}

```javascript
import init, { analyze, parse, isValid } from "qql-wasm";

await init();
console.log(isValid(query));
const statements = parse(query);
const info = analyze(query);
console.log(statements, info.route, info.explain);
```

## Live REST execution

```javascript
import { Client } from "qql-wasm";

const client = new Client("http://localhost:6333", null);
const report = await client.execute(query);
console.log(report);
client.free();
```

Use `setHttpEmbedder` for an OpenAI-compatible embedding endpoint or
`setEmbedder` for a JavaScript host callback.

{% aside type="caution" title="Free owned handles" %}
`Client` and `Stmt` wrap WebAssembly allocations. Call `free()` when the object
is no longer needed, especially in long-lived browser applications.
{% /aside %}

The executable-doc test builds this crate from the current workspace with
`wasm-pack --target nodejs` and parses every `qqlExample`.
